@node Overview
@chapter Overview

@samp{modus-themes-extras.el} extends the @samp{modus-themes} package with automatic theme switching based on the operating system's appearance setting. On macOS@comma{} both the Emacs Mac port and Emacs Plus provide mechanisms to detect whether the system is in light or dark mode; this package uses those mechanisms to load the appropriate theme without manual intervention.

The light and dark themes are configurable via @code{modus-themes-extras-light-theme} and @code{modus-themes-extras-dark-theme}@comma{} defaulting to @code{modus-operandi} and @code{modus-vivendi} respectively. Any theme supported by @code{modus-themes-load-theme} can be used (including @code{ef-themes}).

The package also provides theme-aware face customizations@comma{} particularly for the @samp{highlight-parentheses} package@comma{} ensuring that parenthesis highlighting colors stay consistent with the active Modus theme.

The main capabilities are:

@itemize
@item
@strong{Configurable theme pair.} Specifying which themes to use for light and dark mode (@ref{Variables, , Variables}).
@item
@strong{Manual toggle.} Toggling between the configured light and dark themes (@ref{Toggle, , Toggle}).
@item
@strong{Automatic theme switching.} Detecting the system appearance and loading the configured light or dark theme accordingly@comma{} with support for both Emacs Mac and Emacs Plus distributions (@ref{Conditional theme loading, , Conditional theme loading}).
@item
@strong{Theme-aware face customization.} Setting parenthesis highlighting colors from the Modus palette (@ref{Theme customizations, , Theme customizations}).
@end itemize

@node Variables
@chapter Variables

@vindex modus-themes-extras-light-theme
The user option @code{modus-themes-extras-light-theme} specifies the theme to load when the system is in light mode. It defaults to @code{modus-operandi}.

@vindex modus-themes-extras-dark-theme
The user option @code{modus-themes-extras-dark-theme} specifies the theme to load when the system is in dark mode. It defaults to @code{modus-vivendi}.

Both accept any theme symbol. For example@comma{} to use @code{ef-themes}:

@lisp
(setq modus-themes-extras-light-theme 'ef-maris-light)
(setq modus-themes-extras-dark-theme 'ef-maris-dark)
@end lisp

@node Toggle
@chapter Toggle

@findex modus-themes-extras-toggle
The command @code{modus-themes-extras-toggle} toggles between the configured light and dark themes. It reads @code{modus-themes-extras-light-theme} and @code{modus-themes-extras-dark-theme} at call time@comma{} so changes to those variables take effect immediately without requiring any additional setup.

This replaces the built-in @code{modus-themes-toggle} (which relies on the static @code{modus-themes-to-toggle} list) with a dynamic alternative that always respects the current values of the user options.

@node Conditional theme loading
@chapter Conditional theme loading

@findex modus-themes-extras-load-theme-conditionally
The function @code{modus-themes-extras-load-theme-conditionally} is the main entry point for automatic theme switching. It queries the Emacs distribution using @code{simple-extras-get-emacs-distro} (from @samp{simple-extras.el}) and sets up the appropriate mechanism:

@itemize
@item
For the @strong{Emacs Mac port} (@code{'emacs-mac})@comma{} it immediately calls @code{modus-themes-extras-load-theme-emacs-mac} to read the current system appearance and load the matching theme.
@item
For @strong{Emacs Plus} (@code{'emacs-plus})@comma{} it calls @code{modus-themes-extras-follow-appearance-emacs-plus}@comma{} which loads the theme for the current value of @code{ns-system-appearance} and starts a timer that loads the other theme whenever that value changes.
@end itemize

You would typically call this function once during initialization:

@lisp
(modus-themes-extras-load-theme-conditionally)
@end lisp

@menu
* Emacs Mac port::
* Emacs Plus::
@end menu

@node Emacs Mac port
@section Emacs Mac port

@findex modus-themes-extras-load-theme-emacs-mac
The command @code{modus-themes-extras-load-theme-emacs-mac} reads the current appearance from the @samp{mac-application-state} function. When the appearance is @samp{"NSAppearanceNameAqua"} (light mode)@comma{} it loads @code{modus-themes-extras-light-theme}. When it is @samp{"NSAppearanceNameDarkAqua"} (dark mode)@comma{} it loads @code{modus-themes-extras-dark-theme}. This command can also be called interactively to force a theme refresh.

@node Emacs Plus
@section Emacs Plus

@findex modus-themes-extras-follow-appearance-emacs-plus
@vindex modus-themes-extras-appearance-poll-interval
The function @code{modus-themes-extras-follow-appearance-emacs-plus} loads the theme matching @code{ns-system-appearance} and then checks that variable every @code{modus-themes-extras-appearance-poll-interval} seconds (default 5)@comma{} loading the matching theme whenever it changes. Calling it again replaces the timer rather than adding a second one. A theme you pick by hand@comma{} e.g. with @code{modus-themes-extras-toggle}@comma{} stays until the system appearance next changes.

It deliberately does not use Emacs Plus's @code{ns-system-appearance-change-functions} hook@comma{} and removes this package's function from it. Emacs Plus runs that hook@comma{} followed by a forced redisplay@comma{} inside an AppKit callback that fires while Emacs is waiting for input. After a theme load there@comma{} Emacs stopped running timers and answering the server until the next input event@comma{} so a sunrise switch while the display was off blocked Emacs until the display woke (@samp{emacs/freeze-incidents.org}@comma{} 2026-11-11). With the hook empty@comma{} Emacs Plus only updates @code{ns-system-appearance} and runs nothing else.

@findex modus-themes-extras-load-theme-emacs-plus
The function @code{modus-themes-extras-load-theme-emacs-plus} takes an appearance as a symbol (@code{'light} or @code{'dark}) and loads the corresponding theme from @code{modus-themes-extras-light-theme} or @code{modus-themes-extras-dark-theme}.

